By the Triangle Inequality, a triple $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers belongs to $T$ if and only if $a + b > c,$ $a + c > b,$ and $b + c > a.$

We can write
\begin{align*}
\sum_{(a,b,c) \in T} \frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c} &= \sum_{a = 1}^\infty \sum_{b = 1}^\infty \sum_{c = 1}^\infty \frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c} [a + b > c][a + c > b][b + c > a] \\
&= \sum_{a = 1}^\infty \sum_{b = 1}^\infty \frac{2^a}{3^b} \sum_{c = 1}^\infty \frac{1}{5^c} [a + b > c][a + c > b][b + c > a].
\end{align*}We can visualize the values of $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ which satisfy $a + b > c,$ $a + c > b,$ and $b + c > a$ as follows.  Imagine an $xyz$-coordinate system.  The plane $x + y = z$ cuts the first octant into two regions.  The condition $a + b > c$ says that the point $(a,b,c)$ lies in the region above this plane.  Similarly, $a + c > b$ says that the point $(a,b,c)$ lies to the right of the plane $x + z = y,$ and $b + c > a$ says that the point $(a,b,c)$ lies in front of the plane $y + z = x.$

[asy]
